# Gradual history / push-over-time

History must look like real work across days, not a single-day dump.

## Timeline

- Build and maintain commit history as a dated timeline with natural gaps.
- Example pattern for a greenfield baseline: spread initial work across roughly 3–4 weeks (e.g. 2026-07-01 → 2026-07-24) with gaps (weekends / rest days). Do not force a commit on every calendar day.
- Do not squash an entire project (or a large feature dump) into one calendar day.
- Dates must never be in the future relative to “today” when creating new commits.
- After a gradual baseline is published to GitHub: **forward commits only** (dated now/later). Do not backdate new work into an already-published timeline.
- Do not rewrite / rebase / amend / squash a published gradual timeline afterward.

## Identity (every commit)

```
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Omid Mirzaei
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=omidomirzaei@gmail.com
GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=Omid Mirzaei
GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=omidomirzaei@gmail.com
```

Set both author and committer timestamps when crafting historical timelines (`GIT_AUTHOR_DATE` + `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`).

## Forbidden trailer / branding

- No `Co-authored-by`
- No `Generated-by` / Cursor identities
- No “Made with Cursor” on PRs

## Push hygiene

1. Create and validate locally first (tests, `ENABLE_CHAIN_SIMULATOR=true make verify` → `VERIFY_MVP_OK`, web verify when UI changed).
2. Do **not** push until smoke/tests/hygiene pass **and** the user explicitly says to push.
3. Push as **o-mid** via SSH / `gh` (`git@github.com:o-mid/pooli.git`), not Cursor GitHub App / cloud-agent identity.
4. Goal: GitHub shows gradual ownership over time with a single contributor — Omid Mirzaei / @o-mid.
